WEBVTT

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:02.865
Suppose a shopper picks up one package

00:00:02.865 --> 00:00:05.460
from a shelf of almost identical
products.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.649
How does the store know exactly what to
put on the bill?

00:00:09.789 --> 00:00:12.525
In a grocery store with camera-based
checkout,

00:00:12.525 --> 00:00:16.545
a shopper can take an item off the shelf
without scanning a barcode.

00:00:16.545 --> 00:00:18.557
A store camera records the pickup.

00:00:18.897 --> 00:00:23.595
The checkout system has to turn that
picture into a line in a virtual cart.

00:00:23.595 --> 00:00:27.068
Each line identifies one product in the
store’s catalog.

00:00:27.408 --> 00:00:31.890
The camera supplies an image, often
blurred or partly hidden by a hand.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:36.690
The catalog supplies words: the brand,
the variant and the package size.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:40.997
Matching the image to that text is
called multimodal product retrieval.

00:00:42.137 --> 00:00:44.895
Suppose a product comes in a can and a
box.

00:00:44.895 --> 00:00:48.420
Both descriptions carry the same brand
and product name.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:52.995
The shopper can be charged for the wrong
item if the system confuses the

00:00:52.995 --> 00:00:53.508
packages.

00:00:53.848 --> 00:00:56.175
Here is a real GroceryVision frame.

00:00:56.175 --> 00:00:59.130
The shopper is holding boxed garbanzo
beans.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:03.315
The frozen retriever compares the whole
photograph with the catalog.

00:01:03.315 --> 00:01:08.220
The can scores 0.220, the box 0.218.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.637
The first pass puts a can in the cart.

00:01:10.977 --> 00:01:14.805
A second pass can compare the few
plausible entries more closely.

00:01:14.805 --> 00:01:16.905
That is the job of a reranker.

00:01:16.905 --> 00:01:20.705
But how can it learn the packaging of a
brand the store has just added?

00:01:21.845 --> 00:01:24.975
On its first day, a new product may have
a description

00:01:24.975 --> 00:01:26.475
and one catalog picture.

00:01:26.475 --> 00:01:28.635
The store can already search for it,

00:01:28.635 --> 00:01:31.936
even though nobody has labeled a
checkout photograph of it.

00:01:32.276 --> 00:01:36.540
As people shop, the camera collects
views of the new package under store

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.325
lighting and in different hands.

00:01:38.325 --> 00:01:41.415
These views show what the catalog
picture leaves out.

00:01:41.415 --> 00:01:44.415
The store still has no product label for
each frame.

00:01:44.755 --> 00:01:49.140
ShelfTaught uses these unlabeled views
to train a reranker offline.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:54.045
The first step is deciding which catalog
entry each training frame probably

00:01:54.045 --> 00:01:54.312
shows.

00:01:55.452 --> 00:01:58.530
Consider a separate training photograph
of apples.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.185
The frozen retriever ranks Pink Lady
first.

00:02:01.185 --> 00:02:04.230
We give a larger vision-language model
the frame

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.545
and the ten most plausible descriptions.

00:02:06.885 --> 00:02:08.895
The teacher favors Royal Gala.

00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:12.420
Several candidates remain plausible, so
we ask again,

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.985
this time with their catalog pictures.

00:02:14.985 --> 00:02:16.912
Royal Gala still comes first.

00:02:17.252 --> 00:02:18.690
Both answers are wrong.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.550
This is a Pink Lady apple.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:24.825
Taking a majority vote would turn the
teacher’s mistake into a training

00:02:24.825 --> 00:02:25.081
label.

00:02:26.221 --> 00:02:30.510
A label model estimates an unknown label
from several imperfect signals.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.685
Here it can ask one more question:

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:35.053
which other store frames look like this
one?

00:02:35.393 --> 00:02:39.690
Each dot is a store frame, positioned by
its similarity to two apple

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:40.320
clusters.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.390
The ring marks our photograph.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.735
During fitting, the model combines these
visual groups

00:02:45.735 --> 00:02:49.005
with the retrieval scores and both
teacher answers.

00:02:49.005 --> 00:02:52.396
It also balances assignments across
catalog entries.

00:02:52.736 --> 00:02:55.050
Follow the probability of Pink Lady.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:58.065
It passes ninety percent after the first
update

00:02:58.065 --> 00:03:01.815
and reaches 99.5 percent after fifteen.

00:03:01.815 --> 00:03:04.170
The retriever also named Pink Lady,

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.944
so the frame qualifies as a training
example.

00:03:08.084 --> 00:03:10.440
Those examples train the slow thinker.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.685
It takes the image patches already
computed by the frozen retriever.

00:03:14.685 --> 00:03:18.795
For each patch, it finds the strongest
match among the tokens of a

00:03:18.795 --> 00:03:20.116
candidate description.

00:03:20.456 --> 00:03:23.670
Move to another patch and the best match
can change.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:27.735
Averaging these best matches gives one
score for the description.

00:03:27.735 --> 00:03:30.504
The same calculation scores its
competitors.

00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:33.825
Return to the shopper’s box of beans.

00:03:33.825 --> 00:03:37.530
At checkout, a single candidate can go
straight to the cart.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:41.835
The first choice also stands if the
store has too few training examples for

00:03:41.835 --> 00:03:42.204
it.

00:03:42.544 --> 00:03:46.185
Here three packages remain and there are
enough examples.

00:03:46.185 --> 00:03:51.150
The slow thinker puts the box first with
91.9 percent probability.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:53.985
The cart changes from one can to one
box.

00:03:53.985 --> 00:03:56.005
The offline teacher is finished.

00:03:57.145 --> 00:04:00.885
Across ten brand splits, the share of
correct first choices on

00:04:00.885 --> 00:04:06.420
GroceryVision rises from 78.7 to 91.2
percent.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:11.247
On Klasson it rises from 75.8 to 93.1.

00:04:11.587 --> 00:04:14.310
Retail-786k starts higher.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:17.295
ShelfTaught reaches 95.5 percent,

00:04:17.295 --> 00:04:22.083
while TransCLIP with teacher answers
reaches 97.0 percent there.

00:04:23.223 --> 00:04:26.265
A description lets a new product enter
the catalog.

00:04:26.265 --> 00:04:30.495
Unlabeled views from the store can then
teach the checkout system to make

00:04:30.495 --> 00:04:34.231
the distinction that matters on this
bill: one box of beans.
